ASH for JetBrains IDEs
======================

What this plugin is, and what it deliberately is not
----------------------------------------------------

It runs the ASH command line tool over the open project and turns what ASH reports into editor
highlights. It contains no scanners, no copy of ASH, and no scanner content of any kind. It
invokes the ASH CLI the user installed and reads the two files ASH writes into its output
directory: `reports/ash.sarif` for the findings and `ash_aggregated_results.json` for which
scanners ran.

Tools | Run ASH Security Scan runs a scan in the background. Findings appear as editor errors,
warnings and weak warnings, at the file and line the scanner reported, through an inspection
registered for every file type. The scan ends in a notification that says what was found, what
could not be read, and whether the scan was complete.

So there is nothing here to keep in step with an ASH release. That is the reason the plugin
version is not in pyproject.toml's [tool.commitizen] version_files, and build.gradle.kts
gives the full argument at the point where someone would add it.

The runtime is Kotlin, in src/main/kotlin. It replaced an earlier Java implementation of the
same plugin; the gates, the CI workflow and the planted-secret fixture were kept, and the rest
of this file describes the Kotlin runtime.

Which executable runs
---------------------

AshCliLocator decides, in this order:

  1. A path configured in Settings | Tools | ASH is used AS GIVEN. It is not searched for,
     checked, or replaced by either name below. A value that does not start produces "Could
     not start", not a quiet substitution.
  2. Otherwise `ashx` on PATH.
  3. Otherwise `ash` on PATH, with a notification the first time it happens in an IDE session
     naming the binary that ran. Once rather than per scan, because a balloon on every scan for
     a setup the user may have chosen trains them to dismiss balloons.
  4. Otherwise an error naming both executables and the PATH entries searched, because "not
     found" and "PATH was empty" have different fixes.

Both names are constants in AshCliLocator and nothing else spells either, so renaming the
entry point again is a one-line change. On Windows the `.exe`, `.cmd` and `.bat` launcher forms
are tried as well.

The `ash` name collision on Windows
-----------------------------------

`ash` is also the name of the Almquist shell, which MSYS2 ships on Windows and which has
already shadowed ASH's entry point in this project's CI, producing `Illegal option --`. The
fallback in step 3 makes that collision reachable from this plugin, so every scan is preceded
by `<executable> --version`, and the scan is refused unless the output contains
`awslabs/automated-security-helper`. An exit code cannot distinguish ASH from any other program
that answers `--version`, so the test is a string in ASH's own output. When the output looks
like a shell rejecting an option, the error says so, names MSYS2, and recommends
`automated-security-helper`, ASH's unambiguous entry point.

Both child processes get the null device as stdin. A shell invoked in a way it reads as
interactive READS STDIN, and with a pipe nobody writes or closes it would never exit. The
probe's 30-second deadline bounds anything that ignores EOF.

`--version` and not `-v`: `-v` is `--verbose` in ASH.

What each exit code means here
------------------------------

  0  The scan finished and found nothing actionable. Findings (usually none) are shown.
  1  ASH's ScanIncompleteExit: the scan finished WITHOUT FULL COVERAGE. A selected scanner was
     MISSING or ERROR, no scanner reached a verdict, a converter failed, a rule went
     unevaluated, or a content database was past its bound. fail_on_incomplete_scanners now
     defaults to true, so this is the ordinary result on a host missing some scanner tools.
     When a report was written, its findings are SHOWN, as partial results, with a warning
     titled "ASH scan incomplete" that names the scanners that did not complete. Exit 1 is also
     what a crash produces; with no report on disk it is reported as a failed scan.
  2  The scan finished and found actionable findings. Shown.
  3  Invalid configuration. 4 is a workspace or policy error. Any other code is one this plugin
     does not know. All are failed scans, even if something is on disk -- the readable set is an
     allowlist, so a code ASH adds later is refused until someone decides what it means.

Why exit 1 is shown rather than refused: its findings are real, and hiding them because some
other scanner did not run would make the IDE show less than the command line does. What must
not happen is the partial set reading as the whole answer, so the notification leads with
"ASH reported this scan INCOMPLETE (exit 1)" and says findings from the parts that did not run
are absent.

`coverage_complete`. ASH's MCP server answers "did this scan cover everything" as
`coverage_complete`, computed from the same ScanIncompleteness object that decides exit 1. The
CLI does not write that field into ash_aggregated_results.json, so this plugin cannot read it.
AshScanRunner.Outcome.Completed.coverageComplete answers the same question from what the CLI
does leave: false when ASH exited 1 (ASH's own verdict), false when the status file names a
scanner that did not complete or shows that nothing reached a verdict (which catches a project
that turned fail_on_incomplete_scanners off and so exits 0 over a partial scan), and false when
the status file could not be read. Anything short of true is a warning. When ASH exited 1 and
the status file names nothing -- an unevaluated rule and a stale content database have no
scanner row -- the warning carries the end of ASH's own output, which is where ASH said why.

Both files are deleted before a run, so a report found afterwards was written by this run. If a
delete fails (a read-only output directory), the file's modification time is recorded and
checked after the run, failing closed: a report whose time did not move is refused as the
previous run's, and a stale status file reads as unknown completeness rather than vouching for
this run.

Where the output goes, and the working directory
------------------------------------------------

ASH runs with `--output-dir <project>/.ash/ash_output`, which is ASH's own default location,
so the SARIF a user can open is the one the plugin read. ASH excludes that directory from its
own scan; measured, two consecutive scans of a directory with the previous run's output still
inside it both reported the same three findings on leak.py and nothing under .ash/. The cost is
that the IDE indexes the reports like any other project file. The previous Java implementation
wrote to a temporary directory to avoid that; the Kotlin runtime chose ASH's default instead.

The working directory is the scanned directory, and that is load-bearing: ASH writes SARIF URIs
relative to its working directory, not to `--source-dir`. Measured: a run from the project's
parent recorded `src/leak.py` where a run from the project recorded `leak.py`. Findings are
keyed against the scanned directory, so any other working directory would misplace all of them.

Zero bundled dependencies, and what the plugin relies on instead
----------------------------------------------------------------

A JetBrains plugin distribution zip bundles its RUNTIME classpath into `lib/`. A single
`implementation` dependency therefore puts a third party's jar inside an artifact this project
publishes as a release asset, silently, at package time. packaging/README.md draws that line:
ASH's own code may ship in a published artifact, third-party code never may.

So this plugin bundles nothing but its own jar, and assert-plugin-zip-contents.py is the
enforcement: every jar in lib/ must be named ash-jetbrains*, no loose class files, and no
foreign class inside our own jar (a shade or shadow step would leave the jar count at one and
the boundary crossed anyway).

Two things come from the IntelliJ Platform instead of being bundled, and they are not the same
kind of reliance:

  * The Kotlin standard library. gradle.properties sets kotlin.stdlib.default.dependency=false,
    so the build compiles against and runs on the stdlib the IDE loads for every plugin, which
    JetBrains documents as the supported arrangement. Measured: even with the property on, the
    IntelliJ Platform Gradle plugin left the stdlib out of the zip, so the property is about the
    classpath and not about the bundle. The zip gate holds the bundle either way; an
    `implementation` dependency on any library (measured with org.json) puts its jar in lib/ and
    the gate refuses it by name.
  * Gson, for reading SARIF and the status file. The platform bundles it, so importing it adds
    nothing to the zip. That is a coupling to a library the platform ships but does not promise,
    and it was the reason the Java implementation carried its own JSON parser. Two checks make
    the coupling visible instead of latent: the IDE tests run the parser inside a booted
    platform, so the class resolving at runtime is asserted, and the plugin-verifier job in CI
    resolves every referenced class against the IDE releases recommended() selects, including
    ones newer than the build target. A future IDE without Gson fails that job. In the IDE
    itself the failure would be a NoClassDefFoundError reported as an IDE error, not an empty
    panel that reads as a clean scan.

Build-time dependencies are a different question and are not the concern: the IntelliJ
Platform Gradle plugin, Kotlin's compiler, Gradle, JaCoCo, JUnit, and the roughly one gigabyte
IDE distribution resolved from JetBrains' CDN are all thrown away after the build.

Building and verifying
----------------------

There is no JDK requirement to state beyond 21, and there is no need to install one: the whole
verification runs in a container.

  docker run --rm -v "$PWD":/work -w /work/editors/jetbrains gradle:jdk21 \
      ./verify-in-container.sh

The image runs as root, and the build must not: root ignores file permissions, and the runner
tests for unreadable and undeletable reports fail by design under it, naming root as the cause.
So when started as root the script re-executes as the owner of the checkout (your host uid,
with the mount above) through run-unprivileged.sh, and refuses to run if the checkout itself is
owned by root. CI checks out as root, so it first hands the checkout to the image's `gradle`
user.

That is the same script .github/workflows/ash-jetbrains-ci.yml runs. A verification that exists
twice drifts, and the copy in CI is the one nobody runs by hand, which is why the steps are in
the script rather than in the workflow YAML.

The image is used for its toolset -- a JDK 21, git, python3, curl -- and not for its Gradle: the
build always goes through `./gradlew`, which pins Gradle 9.7.1. The IntelliJ Platform Gradle
plugin refuses to apply on anything older than Gradle 9, and Gradle 9 turns the configuration
cache on by default, which is why gradle.properties turns it back off.

The platform is IntelliJ IDEA Community 2025.2.5, which is also the since-build floor (252).
Not a 2025.3 build: the `ideaIC` artifact is not published from 2025.3 on, because JetBrains
unified the Community and Ultimate distributions. There is no until-build, so the plugin loads on
later IDEs; the plugin-verifier CI job is what checks them.

The gates, and where each one lives
-----------------------------------

All of these are Gradle tasks, so `./gradlew check buildPlugin` gates on a developer's machine
exactly as CI does, and each has one place to relax rather than two.

  assertTestsRan          Every compiled test suite ran and reported, none was skipped, and
                          AnnotationCountTest and AshScanIntegrationTest are among them.
  assertCoverage          Line and branch coverage, the denominator floors, the census, and
                          the exclusion list audit.
  assertDistributionContents
                          Every jar in the built zip was built here, no loose class files, and
                          no foreign class inside our own jar.
  verifyPluginProjectConfiguration and verifyPluginStructure
                          The platform's own two checks, which read META-INF/plugin.xml the way
                          the platform will. Nothing else here would notice a misspelled
                          extension point or a since-build the platform rejects.
  verifyPlugin            The Plugin Verifier's compatibility run against the recommended IDE
                          releases. Not part of `check`, because it downloads one IDE per
                          release; CI runs it as its own job.
  test's own doLast       A zero test count, reported at the moment of the run. Not the gate:
                          with the test sources moved aside, Gradle reports the task NO-SOURCE
                          and runs none of its actions, which is why assertTestsRan is a
                          separate task that reads artifacts.

The tests, and what is real in them
-----------------------------------

The suite runs in the IntelliJ Platform test runtime, and the tests that need a platform extend
BasePlatformTestCase, which boots a real headless Application and Project.

  AshScanIntegrationTest  A scan from the controller (and once from the action, through
                          Settings and the background task) to the highlights in the editor, in
                          a project on disk. The CLI is a shell stub on a real PATH, and what it
                          writes is not hand-written: it replays files a real ASH 3.7.0 run
                          produced over this test's fixture, for exit 2 and for the exit-1
                          incomplete case (src/test/resources/real-cli/README.txt says how). It
                          asserts the planted secret's three findings reach the editor at error
                          severity, the incomplete warning names cfn-nag (MISSING) while the
                          partial findings are still shown, a clean SARIF gives zero highlights,
                          a failed scan clears the previous findings, `ashx` wins over `ash`,
                          `ash` is used with a once-per-session notice when `ashx` is absent, and
                          a configured path is run as given without consulting PATH.
  AnnotationCountTest     The original assertion this plugin exists to make, kept from the Java
                          implementation: a NON-ZERO number of highlights from real ASH SARIF over
                          a fixture carrying AWS's published example secret key, and ZERO from a
                          real clean scan, so an inspection that highlighted unconditionally
                          could not pass.
  AshScanRunnerIdeTest    Process launch, the identity probe, every exit-code arm, the freshness
                          guards, timeouts, and unreadable reports.
  the rest                The parser, the status reader, the path and range mappers, the
                          locator, and the notification text, as plain JUnit tests.

Running the real CLI inside the test was the alternative to the replay. It needs ASH and a
scanner toolchain inside the build container, which would make this suite's verdict depend on
what that image ships.

Coverage, and why the number is not the whole gate
--------------------------------------------------

The coverage standard this repository holds itself to names Python and TypeScript, each gated
independently at 90% and never blended. This plugin is a third language and the standard did not
mention it. assert-coverage.py applies the same 90% here, to line and branch separately, and
prints it as this tree's own number.

The header comment of .github/workflows/ash-typescript-ci.yml explains why a bare percentage is
insufficient: a threshold can be satisfied by measuring less. The cheap way to game a JaCoCo
gate is a widened exclusion pattern, one line in coverage-exclusions.json, so that file is what
the gate audits hardest: every excluded class must be named with a reason, must still be
tracked, must still match a class in the report, and must be under a declared line budget. The
census compares `git ls-files` (`.kt` and `.java` under src/main/kotlin) with the report, so a
source file that is in neither the report nor the exclusion list fails.

The exclusion list is EMPTY, and that is new. The Java implementation excluded its three
platform-facing classes with line budgets, because its tests ran in a plain JVM: under the
platform's test task JaCoCo recorded 344 classes and none of the plugin's, a populated report
whose every value was zero. The cause was that the platform test runtime loads plugin classes
through its own PathClassLoader with no CodeSource location, and JaCoCo skips such classes unless
told otherwise. build.gradle.kts now sets includeNoLocationClasses on the test task (with
jdk.internal.* excluded, the documented pairing), so the inspection, the service, the action and
the settings page are measured like everything else.

Measured on the revision that added the Kotlin runtime: line 99.18% (727/733), branch 95.64%
(571/597), 52 classes gated, none excluded. The floors in build.gradle.kts (50 classes, 700
lines) sit just below, so losing a class fails and a small refactor does not. If JaCoCo ever goes
back to seeing nothing, the denominator stays full and the ratio collapses to 0%, which fails.

instrumentCode is off in build.gradle.kts for a related reason: it rewrites bytecode after
compilation, the tests then load the rewritten classes, and JaCoCo reports 0% for classes whose
bytes do not match the ones the report reads.

What a real ASH report looks like, and defects that came from guessing
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The Kotlin parser was first written against SARIF authored alongside it, and that cost several
defects, all invisible to every test because the fixture encoded the same assumptions as the
code. AshRealReportTest now parses the repository's own tests/test_data/outputs/
ash_aggregated_results.json, a real 7-scanner, 126-result report, and fails loudly if the file
is missing. What was measured there:

  tool.driver.name  = "AWS Labs - Automated Security Helper"   (ASH, not the scanner)
  tool.driver.rules = 0 entries; all 1,264 rules are under tool.extensions[].rules
  runs = 1, results = 126        (85 kind=informational/level=none, 41 kind=fail)
  result.properties.scanner_name = the per-result scanner, on all 126

  * Scanner attribution is per result. ASH puts every scanner in ONE run, so a scanner read
    once per run from the driver labeled every finding with ASH's own name. The driver name is
    kept only as a fallback for a single-tool SARIF.
  * Rule defaults are looked up in the extensions as well as the driver. A driver-only lookup
    was dead code that looked like "this rule declares no default level". Positional lookup
    stays driver-only, because SARIF 3.27.6 defines ruleIndex relative to its own component.
  * grype reports scan-root-relative paths WITH a leading slash (`/poetry.lock`). Treated as
    absolute, such a finding was stored under a key no open file could match: produced,
    counted, and never shown. AshPathResolver now tries the path as written, then joined onto
    the scan root, and only returns a path that exists, so a resolution can move from a
    missing path to an existing one and never the reverse.
  * Suppression is a state, not a severity. 92 of the 126 results are suppressed but only 85
    are level=none; the other 7 are kind=fail with real severities and in-source suppressions,
    which a level filter re-displays. Suppression is checked first, under both the spec's
    field name (`status`) and ASH's (`state`). An absent status on a non-empty suppressions
    array counts as suppressed; `rejected` and `underReview` are shown.
  * Every result lands in exactly one bucket (suppressed, no severity, unlocatable, surfaced),
    and the notification calls it an internal inconsistency if they ever fail to sum.
  * A URI that cannot name a file in this project is refused rather than coerced: a non-file
    scheme, and a file:// URI with a remote authority. One-letter "schemes" are Windows drives.
  * `level` is read as its value. ASH's Python Level is a (str, Enum) mixin whose str() is
    "Level.error", which matches no SARIF level and would fall through to the warning default.

What the status file says, and what it does not
-----------------------------------------------

The per-scanner status is not in the SARIF, and it is not `invocations[].executionSuccessful`.
Measured on a real ASH 3.7.0 run on a host missing most scanner tools: a FAILED scanner reports a
successful invocation, and a MISSING one has no invocation at all. The status lives in
ash_aggregated_results.json, which ASH writes even when `--output-formats sarif` asks for nothing
else. ASH renamed the roster: 3.7.0 writes top-level `scanner_results`, the committed 3.0.0
fixture `metadata.scanner_status`, and both are read.

Only `status` is trusted. The roster's `exit_code` was 0 for every scanner in that run, MISSING
and FAILED included, and `dependencies_satisfied` was true on a MISSING scanner, so neither is
evidence of anything when it reads as healthy. PASSED, FAILED and SKIPPED are complete per
scanner (SKIPPED means not selected), matching ASH's _COMPLETE_SCANNER_STATUSES; anything else,
including a status this plugin cannot read, is incomplete. A run where nothing reached PASSED or
FAILED is reported separately, because every scanner SKIPPED leaves the incomplete set empty
while nothing examined the target. An `excluded` scanner is not reported.

Snapshot tests: a change to what the plugin shows needs a reason
----------------------------------------------------------------

Two suites hold the plugin's UI still, so a change to it fails CI until someone updates the
snapshot on purpose and says why.

The structural suite (src/test/kotlin/.../snapshot, run by `./gradlew check`) renders every
surface as text and compares it with src/test/snapshots/__snapshots__: the inspection's
highlights with their ranges, severities, styles (problem type and text attributes key) and
tooltips; every notification a scan can end in, including the incomplete-scan warning, exit 1
with no report, and the ashx -> ash fallback notice; Settings | Tools | ASH as a component tree;
and the plugin's contributions as the running platform reports them.

The visual suite (src/uiTest, run by ui-test-in-container.sh inside ui-test/Dockerfile's image)
starts a real IDE on an Xvfb display, drives it with Remote-Robot, and compares five scenes
pixel for pixel with the PNGs in src/uiTest/snapshots/__snapshots__: the incomplete-scan
balloon, the editor with an error, a warning and a note highlighted
(src/uiTest/scan/README.txt), the error tooltip, the Problems tool window, and the settings
page. The threshold is zero. Repeated runs in that image render identical pixels, which
ui-test-in-container.sh checks itself when given a run count above 1, as CI does. The plugin has
no tool window or report view of its own, so neither has a scene.

To change a snapshot on purpose:

  1. Run the suite and read the failure: a line diff for text, and for an image a count of
     changed pixels with the rendered image and a diff under build/ui-snapshots/.
  2. Rewrite the snapshots: `./gradlew test -Psnapshot-update` for the structural suite, or
     `ASH_SNAPSHOT_UPDATE=1 bash editors/jetbrains/ui-test-in-container.sh` in the image for the
     visual one. Both refuse when CI or GITHUB_ACTIONS is "true", and no workflow passes them.
  3. Review `git diff`, and look at every changed PNG.
  4. Commit with a trailer saying why the output changed:
     `git commit --trailer "Snapshot-Update: <why the output changed>"`.

.github/scripts/check-editor-snapshot-trailers.py fails a push or pull request in which a file
under a __snapshots__ directory changed in a commit with no Snapshot-Update trailer. A snapshot
no test compares fails too (assert-snapshots-used.py), including one left behind by a deleted
test class. The same rules, flag name and trailer as core ASH's syrupy suite.

Known limitations
-----------------

A scan is a whole-project scan, not a single-file scan, because several of ASH's scanners are
about relationships between files. It runs when asked, from the Tools menu, not on every edit.

Findings with no line-bearing physical location are not shown. They are counted and listed in
the notification, so a run that shows nothing can be told apart from a clean file.

A finding on a line the document no longer has is dropped rather than clamped. The user edits
after the scan, and clamping to the last line would move a security warning onto unrelated code.

DaemonCodeAnalyzer.restart() with no arguments is deprecated from build 253 and the Plugin
Verifier reports it as a deprecated usage there. Its replacement does not exist in 252, the
floor, so using it would raise the floor to silence a warning. AshScanService says so at the
call.

Nothing here publishes
----------------------

There is no publishPlugin task, no token, no Marketplace submission, and no registry of any
kind. The build resolves an IDE, compiles against it, and produces a zip under
build/distributions. Getting that zip to users is a separate decision that has not been made.
